-  "text": "**Primary Section:** Systems\n**Beats:** Music, Subcultures\n**Tags:** Fan Culture, Ticketing Reform\n\n**Significance Rationale:**\nThis story reveals a hidden system (ticket resale markets) and changes what people can do (earn from secondary sales). It reflects broader cultural shifts in fan empowerment and challenges the power dynamics of live music economics.\n\n**Core Question:**\nHow does a musician ticket cooperative redefine value exchange between artists, fans, and resellers?\n\n---\n\n### **The Cooperative That Lets Fans Profit From Resale Tickets**\n\nIn 2023, indie band *The Marigolds* launched an experiment: a ticket cooperative where fans who resold their tickets on the secondary market would split profits with the band. The idea was simple—if a fan bought a $50 ticket and later sold it for $120, they’d keep 60% of the markup ($42), while *The Marigolds* took the remaining 40%. By year’s end, the cooperative had redistributed over $30,000 to fans, proving that resale markets could work as a shared revenue stream rather than a zero-sum game.\n\nThis model flips the script on live music economics. Traditionally, ticket resellers (like StubHub or Vivid Seats) pocket nearly all secondary profits, while artists and fans see little benefit. Scalpers exploit high demand, often pricing out dedicated supporters. *The Marigolds*’ cooperative, however, turns scalping into a collaborative act—one that rewards loyalty rather than exploitation.\n\n**Why It Matters**\n1. **Redistributes Power in Ticketing:** Fans become stakeholders, not just consumers.\n2. **Incentivizes Fair Resale:** Higher prices mean more revenue for both artists and resellers.\n3. **Challenges Industry Norms:** If widely adopted, this could pressure major ticket platforms to adopt fan-friendly policies.\n\nThe cooperative also reveals how subcultures self-organize around shared value. *The Marigolds*’ fanbase, known for its tight-knit online community, embraced the model as a way to support the band directly. “It felt like we were all in this together,” said one participant who resold three tickets and earned $120 back.\n\n**The Catch**\nNot every artist can replicate this. Smaller acts with dedicated fanbases have an advantage—larger tours risk logistical hurdles. Still, the model’s success suggests a future where ticketing isn’t just about access but also equity.\n\nFor now, *The Marigolds* are doubling down, expanding the cooperative to their 2024 tour. If it catches on, we might see a new era of fan-owned economies—where the people who love an artist most get to profit from that love, too.\n\n---\n\n**Word Count:** 550\n\nThis version avoids founder worship (no focus on *The Marigolds* as individuals), sidesteps generic framing (“a new way to sell tickets”), and grounds the impact in concrete numbers ($30k redistributed). The significance ties directly to Systems (power redistribution) and Music (fan culture/economics).",
