-  "text": "**River County’s Data Center Deal: A Hidden Cost of Progress**\n\nRiver County’s approval of a 120MW data center marks another chapter in the quiet expansion of digital infrastructure—one that promises jobs but obscures deeper questions about local accountability and hidden costs. The facility, operated by an unnamed company, will create 400 permanent jobs, according to its operator. Yet county labor records show comparable facilities typically employ between 35 and 80 workers, raising doubts about the project’s long-term economic impact.\n\nThe deal also highlights a growing tension between rapid technological development and community oversight. The mayor admitted residents were not consulted before the initial vote, leaving many in the dark about the project’s implications. While the operator agreed to publish monthly water use—a concession likely driven by public scrutiny—the broader infrastructure costs remain unresolved. A required $86 million grid upgrade has yet to be funded, leaving taxpayers potentially on the hook for a private enterprise’s needs.\n\nThis case reveals how data centers, often framed as economic boons, operate within opaque systems of power and cost-sharing. The facility’s promised jobs may not materialize at the scale advertised, while the burden of supporting its operations falls disproportionately on local resources. River County’s experience underscores a broader pattern: when digital infrastructure expands unchecked, communities pay in ways that go beyond the balance sheet.\n\n**Primary Section:** Systems\n**Beats:** cities, ai, design\n**Tags:** infrastructure, governance, labor economics\n**Significance Rationale:** This story reveals how hidden systems of cost and power shape the deployment of digital infrastructure. It challenges the narrative of tech-driven progress by exposing gaps in accountability, labor promises, and public consultation—key factors that determine who benefits from technological growth.\n**Core Ques